  "body": "## What changed\n\n- `assets/posts/haas.cps` `ff()`: each F word gets a decimal point, in mm and in inch. The function puts its result through `pt()`, the helper that the length words use (#1836). The zero literal is `0.`.\n- The mm feed stays rounded to a whole mm/min. Precision does not change.\n- A positive feed that rounds to zero (under 0.5 mm/min, or under 0.005 in/min) keeps its fraction. Before, it posted `F0`.\n- A positive feed below the inch four-decimal step is refused by name (`msg()`). It is never posted as `F0.`.\n- Dwell P (ms), S, T, H and D stay integers.\n- `assets/posts/AGENTS.md`: the #1836 rule gets one line for the Haas feed word.\n- Decision record: `docs/decisions/3681-haas-feed-decimal-point.md`.\n\nSource: Haas Mill Operator's Manual 96-8000 (2009), Settings, \"77 - Scale Integer F\". The reporter cited rev V. A web check of the same manual agrees with the issue. At the default, an mm control reads an integer F with three implied decimals. The manual recommends a decimal point on each F word.\n\n## Readings (before / after)\n\n- `nc/haas/arc_planes.nc`: `G18 G3 X15. Y0. Z0. I-5. K0. F600` / `... F600.`\n- `golden/bore/haas.nc`: `G86 X10. Y10. Z-12. R2. F120` / `... F120.`\n- `comp.rs` Haas engage: `G1 G42 X0. Y0. Z-2. D2 F500` / `... F500.`\n- Inch, 50.8 mm/min: `F2` / `F2.`\n- 0.3 mm/min: `F0` / `F0.3`\n\nGoldens: 30 Haas files, 658 lines changed. A normalising diff (strip the point from `F<digits>.`) gives 0 lines that differ in anything other than an F word. No non-Haas golden changed. No Haas golden has an F word without a point now.\n\n## Pinning tests\n\nNew: `crates/post/tests/haas_feed_decimal_point.rs`. It was red on the old `haas.cps` (4 of 4 failed) and is green with the fix.\n- `haas_writes_every_mm_feed_word_with_its_point`\n- `haas_writes_every_inch_feed_word_with_its_point`\n- `haas_keeps_the_fraction_of_a_feed_that_rounds_to_zero`\n- `haas_refuses_a_feed_below_its_smallest_inch_step`\n\nThe mm and inch tests check each F word against `F<digits>.<digits>*`. They cover:\n- G1 moves, a plunge and an arc;\n- G31 probe moves (guard, touch, retreat);\n- canned cycles G73, G76, G81, G82, G83, G84, G85, G86 and G89. The test fails if one of these cycles does not post.\n\nUpdated text pins:\n- `crates/post/tests/comp.rs:129`\n- `crates/post/tests/fine_bore_cycle.rs:176` (the context pack did not list this one)\n- `crates/app/tests/fine_bore_cycle.rs:291`\n\n## Gate (final head, -j 6)\n\n- `camcraft-post`, full suite: 129 binaries, 1050 passed, 0 failed.\n- `camcraft-app`: 40 binaries, 323 passed, 0 failed. These are each app test binary that names Haas (`mcp_live` excluded), plus `conformance`, `control_comp`, `program_entry`, `wear_comp`, `post_cycle_feed_spindle`, `example_catalog`, `example_catalog_adaptive` and `example_catalog_edge_work`.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-post -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n- Targeted heavy step (P1 rule): `cargo xtask gate heavy --only \"app heavy: conformance\"`: ok, 493 s. Note: the binary has no `#[ignore]`d tests, so this step ran 0 tests (7 filtered out). The debug `conformance` run above compared the Haas goldens (7 passed).\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- No real Haas control. The manual text is the only check of the control behaviour.\n- `mcp_live` and the UI suites did not run.\n- The light lane did not run.\n- The other 21 post scripts are not in this PR. A follow-up issue holds them.\n\nChangelog: The Haas post writes each feed word with a decimal point (`F600.`), so the feed is correct at each value of Haas Setting 77.\n\nEffort: high (no escalation)\n\nFixes #3681\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
